              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Moisture Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The kit instructions cover it and it is the step most often ignored. A slab passing vapour will push the coating off from beneath regardless of how thoroughly the surface was etched. Tape a plastic square down for 48 hours before you buy anything.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking Too Early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The floor feels hard long before it has fully cured, and hot tyres on an uncured coating lift circular patches where they sat. Respect the full cure time on the box — usually several days — before the car comes back in.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield</strong> is a reasonable product judged for what it is: an affordable, widely available, thin-build water-based kit that makes a light-duty garage floor look considerably better and clean far more easily. It is not a substitute for a professionally installed high-solids floor, and it does not claim to be.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            The results people report track their preparation almost perfectly. Grind rather than rely on the included etch, run a moisture test first, degrease every oil stain, and give it the full cure before parking. Done that way it holds up well; skipped, it peels within a year and the product gets the blame.
          </p>
